1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,820
You trip on the last plank of the

2
00:00:01,820 --> 00:00:03,880
pier and go straight into the lake.

3
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:04,560
Cold.

4
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:05,560
Dark.

5
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:08,020
You open your mouth to scream, and water

6
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:08,820
rushes in.

7
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:11,140
And nothing happens.

8
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:12,560
You don't choke.

9
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:13,720
You don't cough.

10
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:14,960
You just breathe.

11
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:16,040
In.

12
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:17,220
Out.

13
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:19,020
Like the lake is air.

14
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:22,040
Two metres down, you're standing on the bottom.

15
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:24,400
Up on the pier, your friend is losing

16
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:24,740
it.

17
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:25,860
She's been under for a minute!

18
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:26,660
Two minutes!

19
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:27,180
Three!

20
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:28,360
You wave.

21
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:30,420
He does not wave back.

22
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:32,940
So what if you could breathe underwater?

23
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:34,260
Not with gills.

24
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:35,520
With your own lungs.

25
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:37,660
Here's how it would actually work.

26
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:40,680
Night one, you test it in the bathtub.

27
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:41,600
Your mum knocks.

28
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:43,120
Why is it so quiet in there?

29
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:43,680
Bubbles.

30
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:44,600
Fine!

31
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:46,780
And here's what nobody tells you.

32
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:48,652
A litre of air holds about two hundred

33
00:00:48,652 --> 00:00:49,860
and nine millilitres of oxygen.

34
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:52,420
A litre of water holds about six.

35
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:55,360
Water carries roughly thirty-six times less.

36
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:56,820
Your body doesn't care.

37
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:59,348
Sitting still, it burns about two hundred and

38
00:00:59,348 --> 00:01:01,460
fifty millilitres of oxygen every minute.

39
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:04,100
On land, that's about six litres of air.

40
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:06,460
Underwater it's a different story.

41
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:08,720
Your lungs only grab about a quarter of

42
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:09,900
what goes in, at best.

43
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:12,252
So you'd need about one hundred and seventy-two

44
00:01:12,252 --> 00:01:12,900
litres of water.

45
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:14,200
Every minute.

46
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:15,420
While doing nothing.

47
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,900
That's seventeen big buckets of water, through your

48
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,380
chest, every single minute.

49
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:23,040
And water is about eight hundred times denser

50
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:23,300
than air.

51
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:26,060
Each breath isn't a breath.

52
00:01:26,060 --> 00:01:27,220
It's a workout.

53
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:30,180
A hundred and seventy-two kilos through your throat.

54
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:31,800
Every minute.

55
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:34,100
Healthy young men going all out in a

56
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:35,956
breathing test manage about a hundred and forty

57
00:01:35,956 --> 00:01:37,700
litres a minute, and only for a short

58
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:38,020
test.

59
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:40,160
You need more than that just to sit

60
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:40,640
still.

61
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:42,800
So after ten minutes on the bottom of

62
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:44,780
the lake, your arms are heavy and your

63
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:45,760
head is buzzing.

64
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:49,180
You come up gasping, and for once, the

65
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:50,540
air feels amazing.

66
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:53,280
So you study the experts.

67
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:55,920
A trout drifts past you, totally relaxed.

68
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,400
Show-off.

69
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:58,500
Fish don't breathe in and out.

70
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:00,360
Water goes in the mouth, over the gills,

71
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:01,060
and out the sides.

72
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:01,940
One way.

73
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:02,860
Never stopping.

74
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:05,200
And inside the gills, the blood runs the

75
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:06,800
opposite way to the water, so it keeps

76
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:08,520
grabbing oxygen the whole way along.

77
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:10,520
Some textbooks say a fish can pull out

78
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:12,100
up to eighty-five percent of it.

79
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:13,980
Your lungs are a dead end.

80
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:15,760
Same pipe in, same pipe out.

81
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:17,860
Every breath, the old water sitting in your

82
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:19,100
airways mixes with the new.

83
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:20,520
So you learn to cheat.

84
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:21,660
No more big gulps.

85
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:23,800
You swim slowly with your mouth open, like

86
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:25,220
the trout, always moving.

87
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:26,300
It's not perfect.

88
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:28,240
But now you can stay down for an

89
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:28,460
hour.

90
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:29,980
Your friend calls it your fish face.

91
00:02:31,220 --> 00:02:32,520
Then autumn comes.

92
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:34,680
The first time you jump in after summer,

93
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:36,580
your body gasps on its own.

94
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:37,800
That's cold shock.

95
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:40,260
And water pulls heat out of you fast.

96
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:43,300
About twenty-five times faster than air, one safety

97
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:43,820
guide says.

98
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:47,000
Other sources say anywhere from four to thirty-two.

99
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:47,880
Either way, faster.

100
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:50,260
Your hands go numb in minutes.

101
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:53,400
And every breath is cold lake water, straight

102
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:54,220
into your chest.

103
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:55,780
So you buy a wetsuit.

104
00:02:56,260 --> 00:02:57,480
Your friend says you look like a seal.

105
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:58,780
He's not wrong.

106
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:01,160
Then you get curious.

107
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:03,060
How deep can you go?

108
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:06,060
Every ten metres, the water squeezes you with

109
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:07,180
one more atmosphere.

110
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:09,900
At thirty metres, that's four times what you

111
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:10,660
feel at the surface.

112
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:13,480
Divers breathing air down here face a danger

113
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:15,820
called narcosis, one of the biggest risks in

114
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:16,200
diving.

115
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:18,420
And if they come up too fast, bubbles

116
00:03:18,420 --> 00:03:19,280
form in their blood.

117
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:20,140
The bends.

118
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:22,440
But you're not breathing air.

119
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:23,960
You're breathing water.

120
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:25,720
Would that happen to you?

121
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:27,020
Honestly?

122
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:28,260
Nobody knows.

123
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:30,340
Nobody has ever tested it.

124
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:32,340
You decide not to be the first.

125
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:36,040
Then one morning in January, you hear it

126
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:37,020
before you see it.

127
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:38,880
A crack, like a branch snapping.

128
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,680
Your little cousin, out on the ice where

129
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:42,980
the signs say don't.

130
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:43,960
And then he's gone.

131
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:45,100
You don't think.

132
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:47,340
You run, you jump, you're under the ice.

133
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:49,420
It's black down here.

134
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:49,920
Freezing.

135
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:52,840
He's a few metres away, kicking, gasping.

136
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:53,820
That's the cold shock.

137
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:55,060
You grab his jacket.

138
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:56,200
You can't see the hole.

139
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:57,780
Up is ice, ice, ice.

140
00:03:58,500 --> 00:03:59,360
Then you see it.

141
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:01,060
A grey circle of light.

142
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:03,100
You swim, and you push, and he's out.

143
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:04,080
Six minutes.

144
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:05,880
That's how long they say you were under.

145
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:08,140
The paramedics wrap him in a silver blanket.

146
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:09,460
He's shaking, but he's talking.

147
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:10,640
He's going to be fine.

148
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:12,560
Your friend stares at you.

149
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:14,720
How were you down there for six minutes?

150
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:16,440
I held my breath.

151
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:18,180
Liar.

152
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:20,720
That night, your phone buzzes.

153
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:21,660
Unknown number.

154
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:23,020
We saw what you did under the ice.

155
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:24,240
You're not the only one.

156
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:27,660
But could a human really breathe liquid?

157
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:28,980
Scientists have tried.

158
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:31,420
In the nineteen-sixties, mice in a lab breathed

159
00:04:31,420 --> 00:04:33,000
liquid packed with oxygen.

160
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:35,880
In nineteen eighty-nine, doctors in Philadelphia put a

161
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:38,440
special liquid into the lungs of three tiny

162
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:41,040
premature babies, with a ventilator still helping.

163
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,340
Their lungs got easier to inflate.

164
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:46,060
In nineteen ninety-six, a study of thirteen babies

165
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:48,260
saw their blood oxygen jump by a hundred

166
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:49,940
and thirty-eight percent within an hour.

167
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:51,740
Not breathing underwater.

168
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:54,460
But the first time a liquid helped humans

169
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:54,820
breathe.

170
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:56,900
So where's the first place you'd go if

171
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:57,860
you could breathe underwater?

172
00:04:58,240 --> 00:04:58,840
Tell us in the comments.

173
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:01,080
And subscribe, because next time, you find out

174
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:02,060
who sent that message.
